                "text": "**Analyzing the Pattern**\n\nI've been examining the provided training example. It seems the output lengths correspond to the number of consecutive numbers in each input row, but the number itself in the input is not used in the length computation. I suspect the challenge lies in understanding how the output elements are determined.\n\n\n**Discovering the Correlation**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I now believe I've identified the correlation between the arrangement of numbers and their resultant output. The length of consecutive identical numbers in each row dictates the pattern's behavior, with the arrangement of this length, based on the slope of the pattern, determining the output elements. I see the colors 1 and 3 corresponding to horizontal and vertical slopes respectively.\n\n\n**Unveiling the Logic**\n\nI've been drilling down on example 3 and the significance of the \"lines.\" I see patterns forming with diagonals and color changes. It seems I am on the verge of decoding how the boundary between colored cells and 0 cells are being formed. The critical insight is that the boundary appears to be defined by a relationship between row and column indices. The \"line\" can be described by r + c = constant.\n\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Boundaries**\n\nI am now focusing on how the boundaries between the colored cells and the zeroes are determined, looking at Example 3. I observe distinct patterns in the 2s and 3s. I have noticed that the zero cell boundaries can be characterized by c - r <= -2! For row >= 2, this is the pattern.\n\n\n**Investigating the Color Assignment**\n\nI'm now focusing on how colors are assigned, specifically green and blue. I've realized in Example 1, blue's length decreases by 1 for each row below the line, while green's length decreases by 1 for each row above the line. The key seems to be the length of the lines of 2's, 1's and 3's in the examples.\n\n\n**Examining the Color Dynamics**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the color dynamics, especially in Example 3. I've noted a vertical line of '2's. Now I'm analyzing how green (right) and blue (left) expand or contract relative to the length of the 2's line. I observe green growing downwards from its root while blue shrinks upwards. I see it's linked to the start and end rows. This confirms how the color lengths are dictated by boundaries.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Color Assignment**\n\nI'm now clarifying the color assignment logic by tracing color growth and shrinkage across different examples. I'm focusing on why growth or shrinkage occurs on one side of a 'line' versus the other, such as in Examples 1 and 2, which seem to be growing and shrinking on different sides. I see a link in which side grows and shrinks is to the arrangement of consecutive 1s and 3s on the left and right in each example, and I now intend to identify the determining factor.\n\n\n",
